This illustrates why the else condition is important: it handles both the split between subtrees and the case where one target is the ancestor of the other.


Why does the optimal approach take \(O(h)\) time? It moves down only one branch of the tree, processing at most one node per level.
- For a balanced BST, \(h = O(\log n)\), so the optimal approach takes \(O(\log n)\) time.
- For a skewed BST, \(h = O(n)\), so the worst-case time is \(O(n)\).
Important interview point: The optimal iterative approach guarantees \(O(1)\) auxiliary space and uses the BST property to avoid unnecessary traversal. It does not guarantee \(O(\log n)\) time unless the tree is balanced.


Why is the iterative approach better?
- No extra path storage: It doesn't maintain two vectors.
- No backtracking: It never needs to undo a traversal.
- Only one direction per step: It moves left, moves right, or returns the answer.
- Lower auxiliary space: It uses a constant number of pointers and comparisons.
- Uses the BST property: The ordering of values tells us exactly which subtree can contain both nodes.
The brute force approach is useful for understanding the LCA concept in a general binary tree. The optimal approach is better for this problem because the tree is specifically a BST.
